ng a  severe impact on the socio-economic uh  you know status of so many people from  school children to daily wage earners to  um uh the  free trade zone workers who worked in  the factories um it is having an adverse  impact i would say on practically well  nearly all segments of society  now more than 40 members of the  legislature left the governing coalition  this week so now you have a minority  group running the country how much is  the country  you know with the legislature a  democracy as opposed to the rajapaksa  family having most of the power  well even now the problem that we have  is many of the people who are part of  the governing coalition have said that  they have left the governing coalition  and that they are going to function in  parliament as independents  but we still don't know whether when it  comes to certain issues that they would  want with the government in which case  probably why that is why the opposition  is also afraid to test it with a no  confidence motion and the opposition is  also playing what it seems a waiting  game for instance yesterday they made a  decision that today and tomorrow they  would be debating this in parliament  whereas the economic crisis at a point  you know it's going to be meltdown and  in a month or two there will be severe  crisis um  and all economic activity can possibly  shut down uh but yet i think even the  opposition is not putting forward a plan  they are not uh  testing or they are being reluctant to  take responsibility so what is being  proposed is also a caretaker government  but that also brings into play uh many  constitutional questions the question  you asked about you know democracy the  fact is this was in a sense electoral  democracy i would say i mean it is but  it's also electoral authoritarianism in  that through election someone is  governed but that regime is  authoritarian and it also functions a  more it's a family and it functions  through dispensing patronage  so  not ideal  wha